HOLyHammer
==========

HOLyHammer selects premises for a goal, translates the goal and premises to
TPTP, schedules time slices across external provers, and reconstructs a proof
with Metis.  It returns as soon as the first reconstructed proof is verified.

Usage
-----

At least one external prover must be installed.

    load "holyHammer";
    open holyHammer;
    hh ([], ``1 + 1 = 2``);
    holyhammer ``1 + 1 = 2``;

The overall default timeout is 30 seconds.  `set_timeout` is deprecated; use
the option interface instead:

    hhConfig.hh_set ("timeout", "60");
    hhConfig.hh_set ("provers", "e vampire");
    hhConfig.hh_set ("format", "th0");
    hhConfig.hh_set ("type_enc", "mono_native_higher");
    hhConfig.hh_set ("lam_trans", "lifting");
    hhConfig.hh_params ();
    hhConfig.print_params ();

`hh_set` validates values immediately.  `hh_params` returns every effective
option as `(name, value, provenance)`, while `print_params` prints the same
information with short descriptions.  Runtime settings take precedence over
the user or system configuration and environment; use `hh_unset` to remove a
runtime override.

Each call writes its TPTP problems to a fresh directory below the state
directory (`$HOME/.hol4/hammer`, or `$HOL4_HAMMER_DIR`) and deletes it when
the call returns, also on failure or interruption.  With `debug_dir` set,
the problems are kept instead, in `debug_dir/problems`, next to the raw
prover output.

The options are `timeout`, `max_proofs`, `provers`, `slices`, `cores`,
`export_workers`, `filter`, `max_facts`, `format`, `type_enc`, `lam_trans`,
`mono_iters`, `mono_instances`, `minimize`, `preplay_timeout`,
`minimize_timeout`, `preplay_drop_one`, `cache`, `cache_dir`,
`cache_max_entries`, `debug_dir`, and `verbose`.  By default a call prints
only its outcome: the verified tactic or why it failed.  With `verbose` set
it also reports every slice as it starts and ends.

The `timeout` is a wall-clock budget of the proof search, counted from the
launch of the provers.
Every problem is exported before that, on the calling thread, so the export is
not charged to it, except where the measured policy (see `export_mode` in
`hhSchedule.sml`, which states the conditions) overlaps the export with the
search; an empty schedule, a pinned caller and `debug_dir`
always export first.  An overlapped run starts the clock at the launch and the
export then uses the budget: at the soft deadline no slice is started and an
export that no slice waits for any more is cancelled; slices already started
keep their grace period, and at the hard deadline the provers still running
are killed.  Reconstruction is not bounded by the timeout: the proofs the
provers found are all reconstructed, in the order they arrived, so a call
can return after the timeout.  `max_proofs` ends the run whenever that many
proofs have verified; past the soft deadline the first verified proof also
ends it (a proof verified before the deadline included), kills the provers
still running and drops the lists still queued.  Until one has verified, the
lists are reconstructed until one does or all fail.  Each replay takes about
`preplay_timeout` at most, with its compilation; a list costs about two, its
minimisation, which stops at the soft deadline and keeps the shortest verified list, and a final
replay.  An interrupt and a failed export stop
everything at once, `max_proofs` stops the provers and the export (a replay is
not cut); the provers are killed, the exports cancelled and every thread has
exited before the call returns (an export is cut at the end of the problem it
is preparing).

An export error wins over everything but a stop.  The first one to be
reported (after the problems admitted before it have settled) is raised on
the caller, with no result and no ScheduleDone, unless a stop (`max_proofs`,
the deadlines, an interrupt) came first; then it is a late error of a
cancelled export, dropped and printed on stderr like the failure of a
slice.  This holds when a proof has already been verified but `max_proofs`
is not reached: the run raises the error, as the default run does before
any search, and the events already reported, `Verified` included, stay
observable.  The problems of an overlapped run that stops early are partly
exported: a root that the caller owns (`hhSchedule.run_in`) holds only the
files published until then.  The files that are published equal the
blocking export's files for the same counter start, because the export
draws its fresh names from a supply of its own, so that a replay of a proof,
which draws fresh names too, cannot change them, and what the export prints while a replay runs goes to the file
that hides the replay's output.

`export_workers` is the number of threads that finish the
generated problems (the draw-free tail, then format and write) before the
provers start (also settable through the environment variable
`HOL4_HAMMER_EXPORT_WORKERS`).  The default is 2; 1 does the
work on the calling thread, 0 means `cores`, and the count never exceeds
`cores`.  The files written are the same whatever the count.

A prover's lemma list sometimes holds a lemma that sends Metis astray, so
the list cannot be replayed within `preplay_timeout` although a sublist
can.  With `preplay_drop_one` set, as it is by default, each list that
fails to replay is tried again once without each of its lemmas in turn.
These retries run only while no new list waits for reconstruction, and stop
at the timeout (they are not proofs the provers found, and each can cost
two `preplay_timeout`s).  `preplay_timeout` is 2.5 seconds by default.

The `filter` values are:

* `knn`, the existing dependency-aware nearest-neighbour selector;
* `mepo`, a relevance filter based on the goal's constants and types;
* `mash`, the meshed sparse-Naive-Bayes and kNN learner;
* `mesh`, the combined MePo and MaSh ranking; and
* `none`, all available premises in dependency order.

The default value is empty, meaning that every slice uses its tested table
filter.  A nonempty value overrides the filter across the whole schedule.
For example, this runs every selected slice with MePo:

    hhConfig.hh_set ("filter", "mepo");

Use `hhConfig.hh_unset "filter"` to restore the per-slice default.  The old
public references `timeout_glob`, `premise_selection_flag`, and `all_atps`
have been removed; their replacements are the `timeout`, `filter`, and
`provers` options.  `lemmas_glob` has also been removed: integrations should
consume `main_hh_lemmas` directly.

The default 24-slice schedule chooses its premise filter, TPTP format, type
encoding, and lambda translation independently for each slice.  Its first 16
slices retain the kNN schedule; the final eight add MePo, MaSh, and MeSh
rankings.  The built-in slices combine the legacy FOF exporter with typed
`tx0`, `tx0-`, `th0`, and `th1` problems.  The problem generator also
supports `tf0` and `tf1`.  Per-slice choices are validated against the
selected prover before proof search and are included in cache keys and
evaluation journals.

`format`, `type_enc`, and `lam_trans` are schedule-wide overrides; leave
them empty to retain the tested per-slice choices.  Formats are `fof`,
`tf0`, `tf1`, `tx0`, `tx0-`, `th0`, and `th1`.  Type encodings are
`mono_native`, `mono_native_fool`, `mono_native_higher`,
`mono_native_higher_fool`, `poly_native`, `mono_guards`, and
`mono_guards??`; an empty encoding selects the legacy FOF exporter.  Lambda
modes are `lifting`, `combs`, `combs_and_lifting`, and `keep_lams`.
Unsupported prover/format combinations are rejected.  `mono_iters` controls
monomorphization rounds, while `mono_instances` caps new instances; empty,
the default, uses each prover's own cap.

The provers are the constructors of `holyHammer.prover`: `Eprover`,
`Vampire`, `Zipperposition`, and `Z3`.  The `provers` option names them `e`,
`vampire`, `zipperposition`, and `z3`, and `hh_pb` takes a list of them.  Z3
has no scheduler slices yet, so selecting it schedules nothing: a Z3 slice
is added only once an `hhEval` measurement shows that it raises the
portfolio's success rate rather than duplicating existing slices.

Installing provers
------------------

The recommended installation command is:

    tools/download-provers

The end-to-end smoke suite exercises every Phase 2 slice through export,
proof search, TSTP parse-back, and reconstruction.  It also runs cardinality
soundness fixtures through the complete 16-slice public `hh` schedule:

    cd src/holyhammer
    ../../bin/Holmake smoke.exe
    ./smoke.exe

Alternatively, put a supported binary on `PATH`.  HOLyHammer looks for
`eprover-ho` or `eprover`, `vampire`, `zipperposition`, and, for the
schedule-less Z3 entry, `z3_tptp` or `z3`.

The tested versions are:

* E 3.2.5-ho ("Puttabong Moondrop");
* Vampire 5.0.1; and
* Zipperposition 2.1, Isabelle component revision 2.

Executable discovery
--------------------

A prover executable may also be selected in
`$HOME/.hol4/hammer/config` or `$HOLDIR/etc/hammer-config`:

    prover.e.executable = /opt/e/bin/eprover

User configuration takes precedence over system configuration.  Dedicated
environment variables are also supported:

* `HOL4_EPROVER_EXECUTABLE`;
* `HOL4_VAMPIRE_EXECUTABLE`;
* `HOL4_ZIPPERPOSITION_EXECUTABLE`; and
* `HOL4_Z3_EXECUTABLE`.

Discovery checks configuration, the dedicated environment variable, `PATH`,
and versioned installation directories under
`$HOLDIR/src/holyhammer/provers` and `$HOME/.hol4/hammer/provers`.

